A1. These run into bucket shops. ~ SAPS
A4. Usually found with its hands on its face. ~ CLOCK
A8. What they say women have always done, but it's the men who are always broke. ~ PAID
A11. Kind of tree. ~ ELM
A13. A period of time. ~ ERA
A14. An abbreviated crowning glory. ~ BOB
A15. An overgrown hot dog. ~ BOLOGNA
A18. This fellow goes like sixty and takes life very easily. ~ SPEEDER
A21. What little boys like to find in a circus tent. ~ SLIT
A22. Bachelors are fellows who didn't embrace their opportunities: bachelor girls are girls who didn't accept when they had one of these to embrace. ~ OFFER
A24. What shirts get when you send them to the laundry. (This might be lost but it isn't.) ~ TORN
A25. District famous for red tape, thick heads, hot air and scandals (abbr.). ~ DC
A26. An army officer who has no business being out a' night (abbr.). ~ OD
A27. Printer's measure. (This should be hard for the crossword fans!) ~ EM
A29. A very important person. ~ ME
A31. What Americans have to do when they want a drink. ~ ASK
A33. Part of the verb "to have." ~ HAS
A35. Nickname for the rail splitting patron saint of the Republicans. ~ ABE
A38. An undersized animal. ~ RUNT
A40. Acrobats and sheiky dancers are this way. ~ LITHE
A42. "Aunty Everything" is very decidedly this. ~ ANTI
A44. Indefinite article. ~ AN
A45. Peanut catchers. ~ ELEPHANTS
A46. Half an em. ~ EN
A47. A popular drink before prohibition. ~ SODA
A49. Arena for singers. ~ OPERA
A50. A disagreement of newlyweds. ~ SPAT
A52. A little devil. ~ IMP
A54. Metal in the rough. ~ ORE
A55. Exclamation used by all union ghosts. ~ BOO
A56. Degree bestowed by brain factories on serious seniors (abbr.). ~ BA
A57. Diphthong. ~ AE
A59. Pronoun. ~ ME
A60. A musical note. ~ LA
A62. What a bill collector does when he finds the doorbell out of order. ~ RAPS
A64. A get-together meeting of cowboys. (Not the drug store variety.) ~ RODEO
A67. What unsuccessful brokers do when the time comes to buy. ~ SELL
A69. The home of cops and bricklayers. ~ IRELAND
A71. What Englishmen use so that they won't see more at one time than they can understand. ~ MONOCLE
A73. What the live coward did that the dead hero didn't. ~ RAN
A74. A unit of measurement of electrical resistance. ~ OHM
A76. A resort where there is a spring of mineral water. ~ SPA
A77. This is "easy." ~ EASY
A78. This is kneaded. ~ BREAD
A79. A stiff poker. ~ STUD

D1. Feminine persuaders. ~ SOBS
D2. A bird that can hold in its beak enough food for a week, and we don't see how the hellican. ~ PELICAN
D3. You put a penny in this to get a piece of gum. (Try and get it!) ~ SLOT
D5. Eve's evening gown. ~ LEAF
D6. A particle that marks an alternative. ~ OR
D7. A dozen bottles of Mr. Gordon's dry. ~ CASE
D8. Something that gets eloquent in the spring and shot soon after. ~ POET
D9. The region of pain. (When you eat green apples.) ~ ABDOMEN
D10. What sock doctors do. ~ DARN
D12. Matinée gadder (abbr.). ~ MG
D14. Exist. ~ BE
D16. Something that poets who die young never become. ~ OLD
D17. What most people do at after-dinner speeches. ~ NOD
D19. A prefix applied to Volstead when speaking of the good old days. ~ PRE
D20. Before. (Poetic.) ~ ERE
D23. What the early bird probably was. ~ FEATHERED
D26. Correct (abbr.). ~ OK
D28. A schoolboy's alarm clock. ~ MA
D30. When road hog meets road hog. ~ CRASH
D32. This is hot stuff. ~ STEAM
D33. Pet name for a member of a family of omnivorous artiodactyls having superolateral nostrils and phalangigrade feet. ~ HIPPO
D34. What people want to do when a melon is cut. ~ SHARE
D36. A low-down singer. ~ BASSO
D37. Things that girls drop when they want to be kissed. ~ HINTS
D39. Latin word for "one." (Masculine, dative, singular.) ~ UNO
D40. Lion. ~ LEO
D41. Engineers National Association (abbr.). ~ ENA
D43. The pre-prohibition afternoon drink. ~ TEA
D48. You just know the babies wear them. ~ DIAPERS
D51. This animal knows what a whale of a difference just a few scents make. ~ POLECAT
D53. Worser half. ~ PA
D55. To become. ~ BE
D56. In days gone by they used to practice tipping the elbow in this kind of a room. ~ BAR
D58. The recently discovered sea eagle. ~ ERN
D59. The call of a contented cow. ~ MOO
D61. The number of readers who find something funny in JUDGE every week. ~ ALL
D62. What girls do when they get on a trolley car. ~ RIDE
D63. To kill by violence. ~ SLAY
D65. The only byproduct of a packing house they don't seem to find a use for. ~ ODOR
D66. A girl's name. ~ EMMA
D67. Habitual soaks. ~ SOPS
D68. What Brutus wanted his countrymen to do with their ears. ~ LEND
D70. The end of "woman." ~ AN
D72. A fishy province of Eastern Canada (abbr.). ~ NS
D75. A masculine pronoun. (That should cause lots of deep thinking!) ~ HE


